# Files that are executed on the Proxmox host must keep LF endings.
#
# CRLF in a shell script is not cosmetic: bash makes the \r part of the token,
# so `set -u` becomes `set -u\r` ("invalid option"), `do` becomes `do\r`
# ("syntax error near unexpected token"), and paths gain a trailing \r. A
# driver script deployed with CRLF on 2026-08-24 died instantly on every
# launch while reporting a valid PID.
#
# Python and YAML tolerate CRLF, which is why this only ever bites the shell
# scripts — and why it is easy to miss when the same edit "works" for .py.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
